WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.960
Why is a muggle wielding a wand? What&nbsp;
lurks within the room of requirement?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.200
Keep watching for all the secrets hiding&nbsp;
in the latest "Fantastic Beasts" trailer!

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.200
One of the opening shots of the trailer shows&nbsp;
Newt Scamander and his brother Theseus strolling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.880
through a snowy village of tall houses with even&nbsp;
taller chimneys. This is very clearly Hogsmeade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.520
a small village on the outskirts of Hogwarts&nbsp;
which is home to a number of little shops and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.840
several pubs like The Three Broomsticks&nbsp;
and The Hog's Head Inn. It's certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.360
not the first time the village has shown&nbsp;
up in the "Wizarding World" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.960
as it is first introduced in "Harry&nbsp;
Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban."

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.520
Hogwarts students have to get permission from&nbsp;
their parents or guardians to visit Hogsmeade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.920
on their days off, but Harry doesn't get&nbsp;
his form signed by the Dursleys. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.160
why Fred and George Weasley give the Boy Who&nbsp;
Lived the Marauder's Map in the third film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.280
which he uses to sneak down to Hogsmeade through&nbsp;
one of the secret passages out of Hogwarts castle.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.600
It's interesting that Hogsmeade is&nbsp;
Newt and Theseus' first port of call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.760
rather than Hogwarts, but clearly their&nbsp;
latest mission is a dangerous one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.120
Luckily they find someone there who's&nbsp;
able to point them in the right direction.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.400
Newt and Theseus wind up in The Hog's&nbsp;
Head Inn and meet Aberforth Dumbledore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.120
who is played by Richard Coyle. This isn't the&nbsp;
first time Coyle's career has steered him toward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.080
a magical world, as fans will recognize him as&nbsp;
the villainous Father Blackwood from Netflix's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.120
"Chilling Adventures of Sabrina" — which also&nbsp;
follows witches and warlocks. Aberforth previously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.920
appeared in "The Order of the Phoenix," where he's&nbsp;
played by Jim McManus. But Ciaran Hinds took over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.360
the role when Aberforth, who is Albus Dumbledore's&nbsp;
brother, reappeared in "Deathly Hallows Part II."

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.600
Aberforth is clearly happier with a much&nbsp;
quieter life as a bartender, rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.640
Albus Dumbledore's antics with dark wizards. And&nbsp;
that attitude is clear in the trailer as well.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.480
"Off to save the world, are we?"

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.120
Of course, it isn't surprising that the "Wizarding&nbsp;
World" series is fleshing out Dumbledore's family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.400
since the sequel is literally&nbsp;
called "The Secrets of Dumbledore."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.760
Let's not forget that the previous&nbsp;
film, "The Crimes of Grindelwald,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.160
reveals that Credence Barebone is&nbsp;
actually Aurelius Dumbledore. Oh dear.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.400
This family reunion is gonna get messy.

00:02:07.219 --> 00:02:11.760
When we first see "Fantastic Beasts" villain&nbsp;
Grindelwald, now played by Mads Mikkelsen after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.200
he replaced Johnny Depp in the role, he appears to&nbsp;
be extracting the memory of the wizard Yusuf Kama.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.440
The memory extraction spell has popped up&nbsp;
regularly throughout the "Harry Potter" series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.080
It's been used by Dumbledore in the past and was&nbsp;
also deployed in "Fantastic Beasts and Where to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:31.280
Find Them" on Katherine Waterston's character Tina&nbsp;
Goldstein when she's brought to the Death Cell.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.520
Wizards or witches who use the spell will&nbsp;
often store the memories for later review,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.240
using what's known as a Pensieve. Why&nbsp;
Grindelwald needs Kama's memories is unclear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.280
but we know from "The Crimes of&nbsp;
Grindelwald" that Kama is from an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.440
ancient pureblood wizarding family, so&nbsp;
who knows what secrets he might have.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.120
One magical creature above all&nbsp;
that has played a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.800
part in the corner of the wizarding world&nbsp;
we Muggles have been lucky enough to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:00.160
is the elusive phoenix. Seen halfway through the&nbsp;
teaser, the bird on fire swoops over the head of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.120
Credence Barebone with its wingtips slightly&nbsp;
lit. According to Newt Scamander's book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.600
"Fantastic Beasts and Where to Find Them,"&nbsp;
phoenixes nest at the top of mountain peaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.680
suggesting that Barebones has climbed to&nbsp;
this snowy cliff face in search of one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.160
The only concern is for what reason, given&nbsp;
the bird's numerous magical properties.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.880
Phoenix tears possess powerful healing properties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.160
as shown in "Harry Potter and the&nbsp;
Chamber of Secrets." Not only that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:28.080
but any wizard who is lucky enough to have a&nbsp;
phoenix feather at the center of their wand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.120
is a force to be reckoned with. Two feathers&nbsp;
belonging to Dumbledore's own phoenix, Fawkes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.600
eventually wound up in the wands of two of&nbsp;
history's most powerful wizards: Harry Potter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.560
and Lord Voldemort. Keeping that in mind, could&nbsp;
Credence's new encounter actually be the bird that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.040
will end up under the protection of Dumbledore&nbsp;
himself? We're getting fired up to find out.

00:03:48.311 --> 00:03:51.840
The Room of Requirement is one of the most&nbsp;
fascinating parts of "Harry Potter" lore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.760
House Elf Dobby tells Harry about this&nbsp;
secret room, which only appears when a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:00.240
person is in great need of it. It doesn't&nbsp;
appear on any maps, even the Marauder's Map,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.440
and cannot be found unless you know&nbsp;
exactly what you are looking for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:08.000
The room was later used by Dumbledore's Army&nbsp;
in "Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows" when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.560
Neville Longbottom and other members hid out&nbsp;
there after Death Eaters infiltrated Hogwarts.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:14.560
And now the Room of Requirement is back!

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.840
"What is this place?"
"The room we require"

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.720
We will have to wait, though, to see exactly&nbsp;
what this group requires from the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:24.800
and what that big spinning cylinder thing is.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.080
Halfway through, we see one of&nbsp;
the most shocking moments of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.600
trailer — Jacob Kowalski,&nbsp;
a Muggle, is given a wand.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.480
"Dumbledore asked that I give you something."
"Are you kidding me right now?"

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.560
As any "Potter" fan knows, the&nbsp;
wand chooses the wizard, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.240
and Jacob isn't even a wizard.&nbsp;
So how does he have a wand?

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:49.600
Most likely, it's a fake. The plot of "The Secrets&nbsp;
of Dumbledore" focuses on Grindelwald's desire to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.800
destroy the muggle race. This means that during&nbsp;
any encounter with Grindelwald or his followers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:00.000
Jacob would be especially at risk. Wielding a wand&nbsp;
is a clever way to disguise Jacob's non-magical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.280
abilities, and Dumbledore likely planned this.&nbsp;
This theory is further evidenced by the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.200
scene of the trailer, showing Jacob eating&nbsp;
in the Great Hall with some Hogwarts students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:14.240
and refusing to hand it over to a student — a&nbsp;
witch who might be able to out him as a phony.

